    <item>
      <title>Writer Yiyun Li lost both of her sons. When words fell short, she turned to music</title>
      <description>Li won a Pulitzer for her memoir about the suicides of her two sons. In her new novel, &lt;em&gt;Music Against the Night&lt;/em&gt;, she writes about two young prodigies in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.</description>
      <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:41:07 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/13/nx-s1-6000124/yiyun-li-music-against-the-night</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/13/nx-s1-6000124/yiyun-li-music-against-the-night</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/750x920+0+46/resize/750x920!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe3%2F23%2Fea5a754d4a5ba207acd1f62ff6c6%2Fyiyun-li-credit-hannah-yoon-1200.jpg' alt='Yiyun Li won the Pulitzer Prize for her 2025 memoir, <em>Things in Nature Merely Grow.</em>'/><p>Li won a Pulitzer for her memoir about the suicides of her two sons. In her new novel, <em>Music Against the Night</em>, she writes about two young prodigies in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.</p><p>(Image credit: Hannah Yoon)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-6000124' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Terry Gross</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sauna research gathers steam</title>
      <description>Leading researchers in heat exposure are coming together to reach a consensus on what we know — and don&apos;t  — about the health benefits of sauna and its potential for treating and preventing disease.</description>
      <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:39:54 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/12/nx-s1-5977014/sauna-research-gathers-steam</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/12/nx-s1-5977014/sauna-research-gathers-steam</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Leading researchers in heat exposure are coming together to reach a consensus on what we know — and don't  — about the health benefits of sauna and its potential for treating and preventing disease.</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5977014' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Will Stone</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Here&apos;s what to know if you&apos;re unsure about your measles immunity status</title>
      <description>Vaccination is the best way to protect yourself against measles. For most adults, one shot of the MMR is considered enough protection. But there are certain situations that call for a second dose.</description>
      <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 05:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/12/nx-s1-5991904/measles-vaccination-status-outbreak-extra-dose</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/12/nx-s1-5991904/measles-vaccination-status-outbreak-extra-dose</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+0/resize/4800x2700!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe3%2F03%2Fc627607e45a2a96bb711a7def771%2Fnpr-measles-109.jpg' alt='undefined'/><p>Vaccination is the best way to protect yourself against measles. For most adults, one shot of the MMR is considered enough protection. But there are certain situations that call for a second dose.</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5991904' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Maria Godoy</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How one antibiotic morning-after pill came to be widely available in San Francisco</title>
      <description>An antibiotic morning-after pill has been shown to be effective for preventing STIs among gay men, but some doctors worry about antibiotic resistance. This story discusses STIs and their symptoms.</description>
      <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 08:03:51 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5913852/how-one-antibiotic-morning-after-pill-came-to-be-widely-available-in-san-francisco</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5913852/how-one-antibiotic-morning-after-pill-came-to-be-widely-available-in-san-francisco</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>An antibiotic morning-after pill has been shown to be effective for preventing STIs among gay men, but some doctors worry about antibiotic resistance. This story discusses STIs and their symptoms.</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5913852' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>April Dembosky</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Don&apos;t be scared: Being afraid is outright fun, and maybe useful</title>
      <description>Listeners of NPR&apos;s Weekend Edition Sunday may already know that host Ayesha Rascoe loves horror. But why do scream queens (and kings) like her like what&apos;s frightening? The answer may surprise you.</description>
      <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 08:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5973128/dont-be-scared-being-afraid-is-outright-fun-and-maybe-useful</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5973128/dont-be-scared-being-afraid-is-outright-fun-and-maybe-useful</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1367x911+0+0/resize/1367x911!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff4%2Fee%2F536942a74e5586c426d8b455bb3e%2Fmarkoffs-gate.jpg' alt='The gates to Markoff's Haunted Forest in Dickerson, Maryland, is reminiscent of folk horror classics like "The Wicker Man."'/><p>Listeners of NPR's Weekend Edition Sunday may already know that host Ayesha Rascoe loves horror. But why do scream queens (and kings) like her like what's frightening? The answer may surprise you.</p><p>(Image credit: Ryan Benk)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5973128' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Ryan Benk</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>She was a global star: Teen bikes 700 miles carrying injured dad. Where is she now?</title>
      <description>Her epic ride — carrying her injured dad on her $20 hot pink bicycle — made headlines. Promises were made — financial help, training for a cycling career, even a movie deal. Were any of them kept?</description>
      <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 07:28:15 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/g-s1-147267/bicycle-teenager-india-pandemic</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/g-s1-147267/bicycle-teenager-india-pandemic</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1600x1200+0+0/resize/1600x1200!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F27%2F97%2F9a5f653442b6832c364132286e60%2Fjyoti-and-father-2.jpg' alt='Jyoti Kumari holds a photo of her dad, Mohan Paswan. She became a global celebrity after news spread of her 700-mile bicycle ride, carrying her injured father on her bicycle, to get him to their village. Promises were made to reward her heroic feat. Were they kept?'/><p>Her epic ride — carrying her injured dad on her $20 hot pink bicycle — made headlines. Promises were made — financial help, training for a cycling career, even a movie deal. Were any of them kept?</p><p>(Image credit: Deepak Paswan)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=g-s1-147267' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Kamala Thiagarajan</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sweating the details: Sauna&apos;s potential health benefits draw scientists&apos; interest</title>
      <description>A group of international researchers is pushing to rigorously study the potential health benefits of sauna and other heat therapies. They hope to bring scientific legitimacy to the age-old practices.</description>
      <pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 06:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5996655/heat-therapy-sauna-thermoregulation-health-benefits</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/11/nx-s1-5996655/heat-therapy-sauna-thermoregulation-health-benefits</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6300x4200+0+0/resize/6300x4200!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc9%2F3c%2F2e0513c846a39e7557ea290be8aa%2Fthe-well18.jpg' alt='Sauna scientists Dr. Hans Hägglund (from left), Earric Lee and Ashley Mason take a steam  in the Japanese sauna at The Well in Oslo, Norway. They are part of a group of international researchers studying the potential health benefits of sauna and other heat therapies.'/><p>A group of international researchers is pushing to rigorously study the potential health benefits of sauna and other heat therapies. They hope to bring scientific legitimacy to the age-old practices.</p><p>(Image credit: Siv Dolmen for NPR)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5996655' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Will Stone</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ongoing lawsuits allege GLP-1s can cause serious harm. But we don&apos;t know how often</title>
      <description>Scant data about serious adverse effects makes it harder to answer scientific and legal questions about how often the drugs might cause serious harm like blindness or pancreatitis.</description>
      <pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 07:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/10/nx-s1-5996652/glp1-fda-naion-blind-lawsuit-ozempic</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/10/nx-s1-5996652/glp1-fda-naion-blind-lawsuit-ozempic</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4032x3024+0+0/resize/4032x3024!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe8%2F9c%2Fb51836a743e1982e21ce159b9d5a%2Ftodd-engel3.jpeg' alt='Shelley and Todd Engel.'/><p>Scant data about serious adverse effects makes it harder to answer scientific and legal questions about how often the drugs might cause serious harm like blindness or pancreatitis.</p><p>(Image credit: The Engel family)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5996652' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Yuki Noguchi</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Did Russia play by the rules in reporting lab worker&apos;s death?</title>
      <description>A global treaty signed by 197 countries — including Russia — requires reporting any death that could be a sign of an outbreak to come. </description>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:43:45 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/09/g-s1-147295/russia-lab-death-pneumonia-plague</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/09/g-s1-147295/russia-lab-death-pneumonia-plague</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8192x5464+0+0/resize/8192x5464!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F6e%2F2a%2F3ddd11034463ac7c7879271335f8%2Frussia-plague-2298434595.jpg' alt='A woman wearing a face mask walks past the Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and the Far East, founded in the 1930s to monitor strains of dangerous pathogens.'/><p>A global treaty signed by 197 countries — including Russia — requires reporting any death that could be a sign of an outbreak to come. </p><p>(Image credit: Tatyana Makeyeva/AFP)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=g-s1-147295' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Gabrielle Emanuel</dc:creator>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ChatGPT for Teens has special safeguards. A watchdog group finds most don&apos;t work</title>
      <description>A study from Common Sense Media says most safeguards in ChatGPT for Teens failed. The chatbot talks to teens like a friend and doesn&apos;t alert parents when users talk about self-harm and suicide.</description>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 05:00:00 -0400</pubDate>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/09/nx-s1-5995514/chatgpt-openai-teens-mental-health</link>
      <guid>https://www.npr.org/2026/10/09/nx-s1-5995514/chatgpt-openai-teens-mental-health</guid>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<img src='https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5000x3336+0+0/resize/5000x3336!/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2Ff1%2F1e313de8414cad3c0bf3d8a767f2%2Fgettyimages-2271833760.jpg' alt='OpenAI launched ChatGPT for Teens in August, but a watchdog group that studied it recommends that people under 18 not use the artificial intelligence platform.'/><p>A study from Common Sense Media says most safeguards in ChatGPT for Teens failed. The chatbot talks to teens like a friend and doesn't alert parents when users talk about self-harm and suicide.</p><p>(Image credit: Matt Cardy)</p><img src='https://media.npr.org/include/images/tracking/npr-rss-pixel.png?story=nx-s1-5995514' />]]></content:encoded>
      <dc:creator>Rhitu Chatterjee</dc:creator>
    </item>
